        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"unmet\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"The supplied text explicitly states that as of late 2025, no completed Phase III randomized trials have confirmed a reproducible survival benefit of microbiome-based interventions in oncology. It identifies the current body of evidence as relying primarily on Phase I/II trials (e.g., FMT studies NCT03353402, NCT03341143; engineered bacteria trials NCT04167137, NCT03637803) and meta-analyses of these smaller datasets. The text details specific early-phase findings, such as FMT re-sensitizing ~40% of refractory melanoma patients to PD-1 blockade and engineered strains like SYNB1891 showing safety signals in Phase I, but consistently notes the absence of large-scale Phase III efficacy data (OS/PFS) required to fulfill the gap criteria.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The text provides detailed mechanistic context for why such trials are complex, noting that microbial metabolite effects (e.g., butyrate, bile acids) are highly context-dependent on tumor type and local microenvironment (e.g., lactate levels, oxygenation), which complicates the design of broad Phase III trials. It also outlines regulatory hurdles (FDA/EMA guidelines for Live Biotherapeutic Products) and standardization challenges (donor screening, strain potency) that currently limit the progression to large-scale Phase III testing. Additionally, it mentions ongoing or planned dietary intervention trials (e.g., NCT04645680) but does not report them as completed Phase III studies with primary outcome data.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The specific success criterion of identifying a *completed* large-scale Phase III RCT with published primary outcome data (OS/PFS/ORR) for microbiome interventions in ICI-treated solid tumor patients remains unmet. The supplied material confirms the non-existence of such a study within its scope (up to late 2025) but does not provide the actual data from such a trial because none exists yet. The gap regarding the lack of definitive Phase III evidence persists.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Use this material to explicitly state in the literature plan that no completed Phase III RCTs currently exist, citing the review's assertion that evidence is limited to Phase I/II trials and meta-analyses. Summarize the promising Phase I/II results (FMT response rates, engineered bacteria safety) as the current frontier of evidence. Utilize the discussed mechanistic complexities (tumor-specific metabolite effects) and regulatory barriers to explain the delay in launching or completing large-scale Phase III trials. Frame the need for future Phase III studies as a critical next step, using the review's proposed frameworks for patient stratification and standardization as potential design elements for such future trials.\"\n}",
